Well, this relation may be correct, but should be used carefully.
When looking at my logfile, I find about 3/4 of the entries to be ended
by quitting on level 1. Don't know about you out there, but I usually 
start about 10-15 games before I get a character setup that I like (nice
stats and inventory). This is especially true for priests and wizards: 
Who in the world would like to start the game with a spellbook of finger
of death (which s/he either can't reat at all or which will be useless
after 2-3 uses), a spellbook of detect food, a wand of nothing, a wand of 
locking, a scroll of punishment, a scroll of fire or a ring of hunger,
etc. etc.? Ok, so maybe some of the most useless items aren't generated
at the start, but I prefer to have some really useful to start with.

This of course lowers the "success rate" dramatically. If you have read
all the spoilers around, you should be able to ascend sooner or later
with careful play (guess every nethack player lost a lot of promising
characters to his/her own lack of concentration ["Oh, that was a *black*
dragon!"; "No problem, I just get my potion out of my BoH. Oh, where is
my BoH?"; "These few monsters don't threaten me, I'm at full hit points."])
but it will take time. And of course the better you become, the longer
your characters will last. For me, if the character survives the Gnomish
Mines, he usually lasts until the Castle at least. This reduces your no.
of games played compared to a non-spoiled newbie who regularily dies on
levels above 5, but plays several games in a row. 

So, the ascension/death rate isn't really telling that much. If you need
reassurance, ask for an ascension/(death after character reached level 8)
ratio.

YMMV
